From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brian McKenna <brian@brianmckenna.org>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 15:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 25/25] perf(shader): find per-lane descriptor selections in
 their block once per web

LaneSelector found the tuple's last instruction in its block by walking
the whole block and comparing each instruction with every dword of the
tuple, at every per-lane selection of a descriptor web, and
SplitDescriptorWeb then put each selection node in place with
InsertInstructionBefore, which walks the block again to find its place.
A web is built for every split image access and has up to 512
selections; in one title's mesh shaders (blocks of tens of thousands of
instructions) source preparation ran for minutes per shader: five of
eight background compile threads sat in these two walks from a world's
start to the end of the run (stack samples: 211 in LaneSelector's walk,
104 in InsertInstructionBefore's).

A web now indexes a block's instructions the first time a selection is
looked up in it (the blocks don't change until the web is built) and
keeps the list position each node goes before, so the nodes are placed
without searching. The selections, their order and the IR are as
before.

Offline (sa5_check ms, no guest memory, one thread), the title's 526
mesh shaders with a clamped VOP3 v_mad_legacy_f32, 29 of which ran past
30 minutes each: before, the tenth was still running after 13 minutes;
now all 526 take 796 s, the slowest 60 s, and 178 compile, validate and
finalize. The results the old build reached are the same.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
